{"issues":[{"expand":"renderedFields,names,schema,operations,editmeta,changelog,versionedRepresentations","id":"10039","self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/latest/issue/10039","key":"AP-4","fields":{"summary":"Basic Linear Schedule Calculation","issuetype":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issuetype/10037","id":"10037","description":"Stories track functionality or features expressed as user goals.","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/universal_avatar/view/type/issuetype/avatar/10315?size=medium","name":"Story","subtask":false,"avatarId":10315,"entityId":"a9502e0d-867b-4e13-9c45-261e65fe910f","hierarchyLevel":0},"parent":{"id":"10033","key":"AP-1","self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issue/10033","fields":{"summary":"Core Scheduling Engine MVP","status":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/status/10066","description":"","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/","name":"To Do","id":"10066","statusCategory":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/statuscategory/2","id":2,"key":"new","colorName":"blue-gray","name":"To Do"}},"priority":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/priority/1","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/images/icons/priorities/highest_new.svg","name":"Highest","id":"1"},"issuetype":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issuetype/10035","id":"10035","description":"Epics track collections of related bugs, stories, and tasks.","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/universal_avatar/view/type/issuetype/avatar/10307?size=medium","name":"Epic","subtask":false,"avatarId":10307,"entityId":"21b28705-2e2b-43f3-95af-c2d445b6d5a2","hierarchyLevel":1}}},"creator":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/user?accountId=712020%3A87eb1729-fc17-4029-97ee-3780fbdbe2e9","accountId":"712020:87eb1729-fc17-4029-97ee-3780fbdbe2e9","emailAddress":"ataiassistant@gmail.com","avatarUrls":{"48x48":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png","24x24":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png","16x16":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png","32x32":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png"},"displayName":"AI Assistant","active":true,"timeZone":"Europe/Minsk","accountType":"atlassian"},"components":[],"created":"2025-12-10T14:11:28.323+0300","description":"# Solution Design: Basic Linear Schedule Calculation\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\n**Purpose**: This solution design provides a structured approach for implementing basic linear schedule calculation functionality that processes tasks sequentially and calculates start and end dates based on estimates, velocity, and sprint duration.\r\n\r\n**Usage**: This design document outlines the technical architecture, component interactions, and implementation approach for the Basic Linear Schedule Calculation feature (AP-4).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Business Context\r\n\r\n### Problem Statement\r\n\r\n> As a User, I want the system to calculate Start and End dates for each task based on their estimates and the team's velocity, so that I can see when work will be completed.\r\n\r\nCurrently, the system can parse CSV files and extract task information with estimates, but it lacks the capability to calculate when tasks will start and finish based on team velocity and sprint parameters. Users need visibility into project timelines to plan and manage work effectively.\r\n\r\n### Business Objectives\r\n\r\n**Objective 1**: Enable automatic calculation of task start and end dates based on sequential processing and velocity-based duration calculation\r\n\r\n**Objective 2**: Provide clear project timeline visualization through calculated schedule data\r\n\r\n**Objective 3**: Support basic scheduling scenarios with simplified working day assumptions (all days treated as working days)\r\n\r\n### Success Criteria\r\n\r\n**Criterion 1**: System successfully calculates start and end dates for all tasks in sequential order with 100% accuracy based on the formula: Duration = (Estimate / Velocity) * SprintDuration\r\n\r\n**Criterion 2**: Output JSON file contains all original task data plus calculatedStartDate and calculatedEndDate fields in valid ISO date format\r\n\r\n**Criterion 3**: First task uses Project Start Date, subsequent tasks use previous task's end date as their start date\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Solution Architecture\r\n\r\n### High-Level Architecture\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\ngraph TB\r\n    subgraph \"Input Layer\"\r\n        A[CSV File Input]\r\n        B[Schedule Config JSON]\r\n    end\r\n    subgraph \"Processing Layer\"\r\n        C[CSV Parser]\r\n        D[Schedule Calculator]\r\n        E[Date Calculator]\r\n    end\r\n    subgraph \"Output Layer\"\r\n        F[Output JSON Generator]\r\n        G[output.json File]\r\n    end\r\n    A --> C\r\n    B --> D\r\n    C --> D\r\n    D --> E\r\n    E --> F\r\n    F --> G\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Description**: The architecture follows a three-layer approach: Input Layer handles CSV file parsing and schedule configuration loading, Processing Layer performs sequential task scheduling calculations with date arithmetic, and Output Layer generates the final JSON output with calculated dates.\r\n\r\n### Component Architecture\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    Start([Start Processing]) --> LoadConfig{Load Schedule Config}\r\n    LoadConfig -->|Success| ParseCSV{Parse CSV File}\r\n    LoadConfig -->|Error| Error[Handle Config Error]\r\n    ParseCSV -->|Success| ValidateTasks{Validate Tasks}\r\n    ParseCSV -->|Error| Error\r\n    ValidateTasks -->|Valid| InitializeScheduler[Initialize Scheduler]\r\n    ValidateTasks -->|Invalid| Error\r\n    InitializeScheduler --> ProcessSequentially[Process Tasks Sequentially]\r\n    ProcessSequentially --> CalculateDuration[Calculate Duration: Estimate/Velocity * SprintDuration]\r\n    CalculateDuration --> DetermineStartDate{First Task?}\r\n    DetermineStartDate -->|Yes| UseProjectStart[Use Project Start Date]\r\n    DetermineStartDate -->|No| UsePreviousEnd[Use Previous Task End Date]\r\n    UseProjectStart --> CalculateEndDate[Calculate End Date: Start + Duration]\r\n    UsePreviousEnd --> CalculateEndDate\r\n    CalculateEndDate --> AddToOutput[Add Task with Calculated Dates]\r\n    AddToOutput --> MoreTasks{More Tasks?}\r\n    MoreTasks -->|Yes| ProcessSequentially\r\n    MoreTasks -->|No| GenerateJSON[Generate output.json]\r\n    GenerateJSON --> End([End])\r\n    Error --> End\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Description**: The component workflow processes tasks sequentially in CSV order. Each task's duration is calculated using the velocity-based formula, start date is determined from the previous task's end date (or project start for the first task), and end date is calculated by adding duration to start date. All tasks are collected and written to output.json.\r\n\r\n### Data Architecture\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nerDiagram\r\n    TASK ||--o{ SCHEDULED_TASK : \"extends\"\r\n    SCHEDULE_CONFIG ||--|| SCHEDULER : \"configures\"\r\n    SCHEDULER ||--o{ SCHEDULED_TASK : \"generates\"\r\n    \r\n    TASK {\r\n        string id PK\r\n        string title\r\n        number estimate\r\n        string component\r\n        string parentId\r\n        string issueType\r\n    }\r\n    \r\n    SCHEDULED_TASK {\r\n        string id PK\r\n        string title\r\n        number estimate\r\n        string component\r\n        string parentId\r\n        string issueType\r\n        string calculatedStartDate\r\n        string calculatedEndDate\r\n    }\r\n    \r\n    SCHEDULE_CONFIG {\r\n        string projectStartDate\r\n        number sprintDurationDays\r\n        number velocity\r\n    }\r\n    \r\n    SCHEDULER {\r\n        string currentDate\r\n        SCHEDULE_CONFIG config\r\n    }\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Description**: The data model extends the existing Task interface with calculated date fields. ScheduledTask represents a task with calculated start and end dates. ScheduleConfig provides project parameters (start date, sprint duration, velocity) that configure the scheduler. The scheduler maintains current date state as it processes tasks sequentially.\r\n\r\n### Integration Architecture\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\nsequenceDiagram\r\n    participant CLI as CLI Entry Point\r\n    participant Parser as CSV Parser\r\n    participant Config as Schedule Config Manager\r\n    participant Calculator as Schedule Calculator\r\n    participant Output as JSON Output Generator\r\n    \r\n    CLI->>Parser: Parse CSV file\r\n    Parser-->>CLI: Array of Task objects\r\n    CLI->>Config: Load schedule configuration\r\n    Config-->>CLI: ScheduleConfig object\r\n    CLI->>Calculator: Calculate schedule (tasks, config)\r\n    loop For each task in sequence\r\n        Calculator->>Calculator: Calculate duration (Estimate/Velocity * SprintDuration)\r\n        Calculator->>Calculator: Determine start date (previous end or project start)\r\n        Calculator->>Calculator: Calculate end date (start + duration)\r\n    end\r\n    Calculator-->>CLI: Array of ScheduledTask objects\r\n    CLI->>Output: Generate output.json (scheduled tasks)\r\n    Output-->>CLI: output.json file written\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Description**: The integration flow shows how components interact: CLI orchestrates the process, CSV Parser extracts tasks, Schedule Config Manager provides configuration, Schedule Calculator performs sequential date calculations, and JSON Output Generator writes the final result. All interactions are synchronous for predictable scheduling logic.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Technical Design\r\n\r\n### Technology Stack\r\n\r\n**Runtime & Language:**\r\n- Node.js - Runtime environment for CLI application\r\n- TypeScript - Type-safe development with strict mode\r\n\r\n**Libraries & Frameworks:**\r\n- csv-parse - CSV file parsing (already in use)\r\n- Built-in Date/Time handling - Date arithmetic and ISO format conversion\r\n\r\n**Tools & Infrastructure:**\r\n- TypeScript Compiler - Type checking and compilation\r\n- Vitest - Unit testing framework (already configured)\r\n\r\n### Design Patterns\r\n\r\n**Pattern 1: Sequential Processor Pattern** - Tasks are processed one-by-one in strict CSV order, with each task's start date dependent on the previous task's end date. This ensures deterministic scheduling.\r\n\r\n**Pattern 2: Immutable Data Pattern** - Task objects are treated as immutable during calculation. New ScheduledTask objects are created with calculated dates rather than mutating existing Task objects.\r\n\r\n**Pattern 3: Functional Calculation Pattern** - Duration and date calculations are implemented as pure functions (e.g., `calculateDuration(estimate, velocity, sprintDuration)`, `calculateEndDate(startDate, duration)`), ensuring predictability and testability.\r\n\r\n### Security Considerations\r\n\r\n**Security measure 1**: Input validation for CSV file paths and configuration files to prevent path traversal attacks\r\n\r\n**Security measure 2**: File size limits for CSV input to prevent memory exhaustion (already implemented in csv-parser)\r\n\r\n**Security measure 3**: Validation of date formats and numeric values in configuration to prevent injection or calculation errors\r\n\r\n### Performance Considerations\r\n\r\n**Performance optimization 1**: Sequential processing is O(n) where n is the number of tasks, which is optimal for linear scheduling requirements\r\n\r\n**Performance optimization 2**: In-memory processing of tasks avoids repeated file I/O during calculation phase\r\n\r\n**Performance optimization 3**: Date calculations use native JavaScript Date objects for efficient arithmetic operations\r\n\r\n### Scalability Considerations\r\n\r\n**Scalability approach 1**: Current design supports processing of CSV files with reasonable task counts (hundreds to low thousands of tasks) efficiently\r\n\r\n**Scalability approach 2**: For very large task lists, the sequential processing approach can be extended with streaming or batch processing if needed in future iterations\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Development Scope Decomposition\r\n\r\n### Decomposition Strategy\r\n\r\n**Important: Decompose based on logical technical boundaries, not artificial module types (e.g., Core/API/UI). Only decompose if the scope is too large for a single implementation.**\r\n\r\n**Approach: Single Solution Design**\r\n\r\n**If scope is manageable, keep as a single solution design without decomposition.**\r\n\r\nThe Basic Linear Schedule Calculation feature has a well-defined, focused scope that can be implemented as a single cohesive unit. The functionality includes:\r\n- Schedule calculation logic (duration, start date, end date)\r\n- Integration with existing CSV parser and configuration system\r\n- JSON output generation\r\n\r\nThis scope is appropriate for a single implementation iteration without artificial decomposition.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Development Workflow\r\n\r\n```mermaid\r\ngraph LR\r\n    A[Planning] --> B[Design]\r\n    B --> C[Development]\r\n    C --> D[Code Review]\r\n    D --> E[Testing]\r\n    E --> F{Pass?}\r\n    F -->|Yes| G[Integration]\r\n    G --> H[Validation]\r\n    F -->|No| C\r\n    H --> I[Documentation]\r\n    I --> J[Deploy]\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Description**: Standard development workflow: Planning and Design phases establish requirements and architecture, Development implements the schedule calculator and output generator, Code Review ensures quality, Testing validates calculations and edge cases, Integration combines with existing CSV parser, Validation confirms end-to-end functionality, Documentation updates user guides, and Deploy releases the feature.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Dependencies & Risks\r\n\r\n### Dependencies\r\n\r\n**External Dependency 1**: Schedule configuration file (config.json) must be provided with valid projectStartDate, sprintDurationDays, and velocity values - Impact if delayed: Feature cannot function without configuration\r\n\r\n**Internal Dependency 1**: Existing CSV parser must continue to provide Task objects in CSV file order - Impact if delayed: Sequential processing order may be incorrect\r\n\r\n**Internal Dependency 2**: Schedule configuration manager (schedule-config.ts) must be initialized before schedule calculation - Impact if delayed: Configuration values will be unavailable\r\n\r\n### Risks & Mitigation\r\n\r\n**Risk 1**: Division by zero if velocity is set to 0 in configuration\r\n\r\n**Probability**: Low\r\n\r\n**Impact**: High\r\n\r\n**Mitigation**: Add validation in schedule configuration validator to ensure velocity > 0, and add runtime check in calculator with clear error message\r\n\r\n**Risk 2**: Invalid date calculations if project start date is malformed\r\n\r\n**Probability**: Medium\r\n\r\n**Impact**: High\r\n\r\n**Mitigation**: Use schedule configuration validator to ensure projectStartDate is valid ISO date format (YYYY-MM-DD), and add date parsing validation in calculator\r\n\r\n**Risk 3**: Tasks with missing estimates causing incorrect duration calculations\r\n\r\n**Probability**: Medium\r\n\r\n**Impact**: Medium\r\n\r\n**Mitigation**: CSV parser already defaults missing estimates to 1, but add explicit validation in schedule calculator to ensure all tasks have valid estimates before processing\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Testing Strategy\r\n\r\n### Unit Testing\r\n\r\n**Coverage target**: 90%+ for schedule calculation logic\r\n\r\n**Key areas**: \r\n- Duration calculation formula: (Estimate / Velocity) * SprintDuration\r\n- Start date determination (first task vs. subsequent tasks)\r\n- End date calculation: Start Date + Duration\r\n- Date arithmetic with working days (all days treated as working days)\r\n- Edge cases: zero estimates, very large estimates, date boundary conditions\r\n\r\n### Integration Testing\r\n\r\n**Scope**: End-to-end flow from CSV input through schedule calculation to JSON output\r\n\r\n**Key scenarios**:\r\n- Complete workflow: CSV file → Parsed tasks → Schedule calculation → output.json generation\r\n- Configuration loading and validation\r\n- Sequential task processing order preservation\r\n- Output JSON structure validation (all original fields + calculated dates)\r\n\r\n### End-to-End Testing\r\n\r\n**Scope**: Full CLI command execution with real CSV files and configuration\r\n\r\n**Key user journeys**:\r\n- User provides CSV file with tasks, estimates, and valid config.json → System generates output.json with calculated dates\r\n- User provides CSV file with missing estimates → System uses defaults and calculates schedule\r\n- User provides invalid configuration → System displays clear error message\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Deployment Plan\r\n\r\n### Environments\r\n\r\n**Development**: Local development environment with test CSV files and configuration\r\n\r\n**Staging**: N/A (CLI tool, no separate staging environment required)\r\n\r\n**Production**: N/A (CLI tool distributed as npm package or executable)\r\n\r\n### Deployment Strategy\r\n\r\n**Approach**: Direct deployment as CLI tool (no blue-green or canary deployment needed)\r\n\r\n**Rollback plan**: Version control allows reverting to previous version if issues are discovered\r\n\r\n**Monitoring**: N/A (CLI tool, no runtime monitoring required. User feedback and testing validate functionality)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Documentation Requirements\r\n\r\n**API Documentation**: N/A (No external API, internal TypeScript interfaces documented with TSDoc)\r\n\r\n**User Documentation**: Update README with usage instructions for schedule calculation feature, including CSV format requirements, configuration file format, and output.json structure\r\n\r\n**Technical Documentation**: TSDoc comments for all exported functions and interfaces, including calculation formulas and date handling logic\r\n\r\n**Runbooks**: N/A (CLI tool, no operational runbooks required)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Timeline & Milestones\r\n\r\n**Milestone 1**: Schedule Calculator Implementation - Target: Sprint 1\r\n\r\n**Milestone 2**: Integration with CSV Parser and Configuration System - Target: Sprint 1\r\n\r\n**Milestone 3**: Output JSON Generation and End-to-End Testing - Target: Sprint 1\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Open Questions\r\n\r\n**Question 1**: Should the system support fractional days in duration calculations, or round to whole days? - Owner: Product Owner - Status: To be clarified (assume fractional days supported for MVP)\r\n\r\n**Question 2**: What date format should be used for calculatedStartDate and calculatedEndDate in output.json? - Owner: Technical Lead - Status: Resolved (ISO format YYYY-MM-DD per requirements)\r\n\r\n**Question 3**: Should the system validate that calculated end dates don't exceed a maximum project end date? - Owner: Product Owner - Status: Out of scope for MVP (no maximum end date validation)\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Appendix\r\n\r\n### References\r\n\r\n**[Reference 1]**: [Solution design template](https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AC/pages/25133058/Solution+design+template)\r\n\r\n**[Reference 2]**: [JIRA Template Story](https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AC/pages/786465/JIRA+Template+Story)\r\n\r\n**[Reference 3]**: Existing codebase - src/models/Task.ts, src/parsers/csv-parser.ts, src/config/schedule-config.ts\r\n\r\n### Glossary\r\n\r\n**Velocity**: Story points (or estimate units) that a team can complete per sprint\r\n\r\n**Sprint Duration**: Number of days in a sprint (typically 7, 10, or 14 days)\r\n\r\n**Sequential Processing**: Processing tasks one after another in the order they appear in the CSV file, with each task starting when the previous task ends\r\n\r\n**Working Days**: Days available for work. For MVP, all days (including weekends) are treated as working days\r\n\r\n**Duration**: Time required to complete a task, calculated as (Estimate / Velocity) * SprintDuration\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Story AC Coverage\r\n\r\n### Functional Criteria Coverage\r\n\r\n- **AC1**: Given a CSV file with tasks, estimates, velocity, and sprint duration, When the system processes the file, Then it calculates Start and End dates for each task in sequential order\r\n  - **Covered by**: Schedule Calculator component with sequential processing logic\r\n\r\n- **AC2**: Given a task with Estimate = 5 points, Velocity = 10/week, and SprintDuration = 7 days, When duration is calculated, Then the result is 0.5 weeks (3.5 days) as per the formula: (5/10) * 7 = 3.5 days\r\n  - **Covered by**: Duration calculation function: `calculateDuration(estimate, velocity, sprintDuration)`\r\n\r\n- **AC3**: Given the first task in the sequence, When Start Date is calculated, Then it uses the Project Start Date instead of a previous task's End Date\r\n  - **Covered by**: Start date determination logic with conditional check for first task\r\n\r\n- **AC4**: Given any task after the first, When Start Date is calculated, Then it uses the End Date of the previous task\r\n  - **Covered by**: Sequential processing logic that tracks previous task's end date\r\n\r\n- **AC5**: Given all calculated dates, When the output.json file is generated, Then it contains all original task data plus calculatedStartDate and calculatedEndDate fields\r\n  - **Covered by**: JSON Output Generator component that merges original task data with calculated dates\r\n\r\n- **AC6**: Given weekend and holiday dates, When dates are calculated, Then the system treats all days as working days (no skipping of weekends or holidays)\r\n  - **Covered by**: Date calculation logic that uses simple date arithmetic without calendar awareness\r\n\r\n- **AC7**: All business rules implemented and validated\r\n  - **Covered by**: Complete implementation of sequential processing, velocity-based duration, and date calculations\r\n\r\n- **AC8**: All error cases handled with appropriate user-friendly messages (invalid CSV format, missing required fields, division by zero for velocity, etc.)\r\n  - **Covered by**: Error handling in CSV parser (existing), configuration validator, and schedule calculator with clear error messages\r\n\r\n### User Experience Criteria Coverage\r\n\r\n- **UX1**: User can provide a CSV file and receive a complete output.json file with calculated dates\r\n  - **Covered by**: End-to-end workflow from CSV input to JSON output\r\n\r\n- **UX2**: Error messages are clear and actionable if input data is invalid\r\n  - **Covered by**: Error handling with descriptive messages for configuration errors, validation failures, and calculation issues\r\n\r\n- **UX3**: Processing completes successfully and generates output file\r\n  - **Covered by**: Complete implementation with file I/O for output.json generation\r\n\r\n### Quality Criteria Coverage\r\n\r\n- **QC1**: Feature works as expected and produces accurate date calculations\r\n  - **Covered by**: Unit tests for calculation formulas and integration tests for end-to-end flow\r\n\r\n- **QC2**: Output JSON file format is valid and contains all required fields\r\n  - **Covered by**: JSON schema validation and integration tests\r\n\r\n- **QC3**: Manual verification passes: If velocity is 10/week and Task A is 5 points, it takes 0.5 weeks (3.5 days)\r\n  - **Covered by**: Unit test with exact scenario: Estimate=5, Velocity=10, SprintDuration=7 → Duration=3.5 days\r\n\r\n- **QC4**: User documentation updated (if applicable)\r\n  - **Covered by**: Documentation requirements section specifying README updates","reporter":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/user?accountId=712020%3A87eb1729-fc17-4029-97ee-378